WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
Nobody has seen a wormhole.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.000
Nobody has produced one.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.420
Nobody knows if it is just beautiful&nbsp;
math, but without a physical reality.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.540
Well, we'll take the world's best quantum computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.900
and see if we can map that&nbsp;
into building a wormhole.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.440
And then it became just at&nbsp;
the edge of what's possible.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.480
I think the discovery of the Higgs, which&nbsp;
came about 10 years ago, was up to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.340
point the most exciting point of my entire&nbsp;
career. This, to me, is equally exciting.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:40.260
It can begin to explore quantum states that&nbsp;
are very hard to realize in nature directly.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:46.200
Now, the wormhole becomes traversable.&nbsp;
It opens. You really can go through.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:01:02.100
So, the story begins in 1935 when Einstein wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:08.640
a paper with Rosen, where the first&nbsp;
time Einstein-Rosen bridges appear.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:17.220
And these are bridges that connect two&nbsp;
seemingly unconnectable pieces of space-time.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:26.640
ER–Einstein and Rosen–they pointed out that&nbsp;
solutions to general relativity allowed for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.100
black holes, which are connected, but with&nbsp;
a kind of bridge–what we call a wormhole.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.520
But that wormhole has the&nbsp;
property of being non-traversable.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.160
Wormhole is a very unstable passage.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.100
It opens and it closes and it gets&nbsp;
chaotic and it gets destroyed very fast.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.300
So Einstein, when he proposed the&nbsp;
wormhole, he was very frustrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.320
because you couldn't actually go through a&nbsp;
wormhole. And he was able to realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.740
himself. And so, what good is a wormhole&nbsp;
if you can't put anything through it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.880
And it turns out that that's because he&nbsp;
wasn't putting in the quantum physics.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.040
1935: same year, another paper.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.740
The EPR paper, which is Einstein, Rosen, Podolsky.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:22.020
EPR is about quantum entanglement and what&nbsp;
Einstein called spooky action at a distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.460
something he was not happy with.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:31.620
Entanglement is this property when subatomic&nbsp;
particles–they are partners. Even if you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.780
them apart, and take them at the other edge of&nbsp;
the universe, they hold information that if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.840
measure the one, you know what the other will&nbsp;
be. So entanglement is a strong correlation.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.960
Einstein didn't like quantum entanglement,&nbsp;
and this made it very challenging for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:03:00.120
to understand how to reconcile quantum&nbsp;
physics and gravitational physics. So it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:08.580
has been a challenge over decades&nbsp;
to understand better how quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.020
physics and general relativity fit&nbsp;
together into a coherent picture.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.200
Einstein's dream was to figure&nbsp;
out how to connect them.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.260
There was no reason to think at the time or&nbsp;
for decades afterward that those two papers had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.060
anything to do with one another, apart&nbsp;
from there being some authors in common.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:43.500
And then comes 2013: Maldecena and&nbsp;
Lenny Susskind. They say ER equals EPR.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.560
They are the same. This is very daring.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.680
Wormholes and entanglement are the same thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.480
That was totally unexpected. One of them&nbsp;
has to do with black holes. One of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.020
has to do with quantum mechanics. Didn't seem&nbsp;
like they had anything to do with each other.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.160
A lot of times in physics, you can have&nbsp;
two different descriptions of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.220
thing. And we call that a duality. It's just&nbsp;
sort of a remapping of the same physics.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.860
The basic idea was that when you&nbsp;
have quantum entanglement between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.900
two black holes, necessarily there&nbsp;
will be a wormhole connecting them.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.640
So there we have a picture of quantum gravity.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:12.060
We have this dictionary that relates gravitational&nbsp;
phenomena to ordinary quantum physics.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.660
You can tackle the same physics from these&nbsp;
two different angles, and that's one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:19.740
most concrete paths forward we have in&nbsp;
building a full theory of quantum gravity.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:26.520
The physics of the black holes, we think,&nbsp;
is physics of quantum gravity. But we cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.720
send an experiment in a&nbsp;
black hole and validate that.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.800
So instead we can ask, can&nbsp;
there be quantum systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.400
whose dual description looks like space-time?

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.180
I think it might have actually been during a talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.920
by Juan Maldacena about ER = EPR&nbsp;
that I started to wonder to myself:&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.440
Could traversable wormholes exist? And people&nbsp;
thought it was fascinating as an exotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.980
configuration of space-time. But I think many&nbsp;
people probably suspected that it couldn't work.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.560
So we saw that it could.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.580
One of the magical things about quantum&nbsp;
mechanics is you can actually have negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.840
energy. And this allows you to do things that&nbsp;
you would've thought otherwise were impossible.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.720
Daniel Jafferis showed was that if you introduce&nbsp;
what’s known as a negative energy shockwave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.220
you can support your wormhole and get something&nbsp;
from one end and have it come out the other.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:31.800
Now, I'm an experimentalist, so I look at that&nbsp;
and I ... All right, what can I do about that?

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.560
And so Maria and I were thinking, we have&nbsp;
the ability to do things in quantum in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.720
the laboratory. We're physicists. So could we&nbsp;
imagine actually doing something with wormholes?

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.640
It almost occurred to us immediately that&nbsp;
we should be using the best quantum system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.880
that we know how to manipulate. And&nbsp;
what is that? It's a quantum computer.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:58.260
Quantum computers speak a different&nbsp;
language than the ordinary language of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:02.760
bits. A bit–you look at it, okay,&nbsp;
it's a zero or one, that's it.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:09.480
The correlations among qubits are richer&nbsp;
and a lot more interesting than correlations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:14.880
among bits. It’s this language of quantum&nbsp;
entanglement. And so there's a world out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.500
this extravagant quantum world, which&nbsp;
we just haven't been able to explore.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:27.780
I've worked on a few projects at Google Quantum,&nbsp;
and got to get to know the device a bit.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.800
The Google device is the best&nbsp;
quantum computer that is around.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:39.780
All the magic happens in this little chip. You&nbsp;
have the actual qubits as these superconducting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:43.620
pieces of aluminum on a silicon chip. And&nbsp;
that's where the entanglement happens.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.620
We had access to the Google quantum&nbsp;
computer. And so Maria and Daniel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:56.520
Joe and I said, “We have a proposal to try to&nbsp;
observe a wormhole on the quantum computer."

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.420
And to put the proposal together, it&nbsp;
was obvious that these are the people.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.760
In physics, you have to have the courage of&nbsp;
your own convictions. And you say, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.600
maybe it won't work the way that I thought,&nbsp;
but we'll learn something by trying." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.900
that's really the attitude we had going&nbsp;
into this. I don't think we really thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:16.620
it was going to work, but we thought we would&nbsp;
learn a lot by seeing how far you could get.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.920
They didn't say, "You're crazy.&nbsp;
Let's do something else."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:22.680
They said, "Okay, you are crazy. Let's do it."

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:45.420
You can use the holographic duality to&nbsp;
create the quantum version of the system.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:51.120
We look instead at this set of&nbsp;
entangled qubits and we evolve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.320
it in a way that has exactly the same&nbsp;
physics as the gravitational wormhole.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:00.120
And so, on the quantum computer,&nbsp;
we can directly send a qubit from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:02.520
one side of the wormhole and&nbsp;
watch it come out the other.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:10.200
If you take the physical system that theoretical&nbsp;
physicists say, "this is a wormhole," and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.060
work out pencil-and-paper, "how good of&nbsp;
a quantum computer do I need?" You get an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:18.540
answer that's like, "a quantum computer&nbsp;
10 years from now." It looks hopeless.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:22.020
But we thought, “Let's try.”

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:33.000
We are here in our GQ2 lab… the controlled signals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.980
actually are produced in these&nbsp;
racks sitting next to the cryostat.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.660
When you try to just run a wormhole&nbsp;
as the theorists have prepared it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:46.320
on a quantum computer, you find that you&nbsp;
need to implement this very large system.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:51.420
So that's big. That's a big fat problem.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.240
We have to do what physicists&nbsp;
call coarse-graining. You want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.040
to preserve certain features–in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.820
gravitational features, while throwing&nbsp;
away all the unnecessary components.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:08.460
We knew we needed to get to a smaller system size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:11.700
and so we were banging our heads&nbsp;
against it for a year or something.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.280
And then finally, you come up&nbsp;
with a weird idea that might work.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:25.380
The idea of rewriting it as a neural network&nbsp;
is something that's out there. It's a weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:29.820
idea because you usually don't think of&nbsp;
physical wormholes as neural networks.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.660
The wormhole itself has these many&nbsp;
parameters that we treat as neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:36.900
network weights. We then have a data&nbsp;
set that we're going to train on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:41.160
which is the dynamics that we want to&nbsp;
observe that correspond to wormhole.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:43.920
It sounds like a generic&nbsp;
machine-learning problem now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.340
and we can just plug it straight&nbsp;
into the machine-learning toolbox.

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:50.580
I proposed the idea and tried it out.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.832
The original system has 210 terms.

00:10:53.832 --> 00:10:53.880
And we found out that we can do this with seven.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:11:00.780
The machine learning managed to keep all of&nbsp;
this intact while making a much smaller system.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:07.440
It’s still baby enough that it fits in the crib.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:12.960
That was the first moment where,&nbsp;
like, "It might be possible. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:16.200
whole wormhole thing we've been talking about&nbsp;
for two years, maybe we can actually do it."

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:37.560
At probably like two in the morning,&nbsp;
I was running these quantum circuits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:41.400
We're looking for a peak. That's the&nbsp;
qubit making it through the wormhole.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:47.340
I'm watching the data as it comes out and&nbsp;
there's a little bit of a noisy peak. And at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:51.300
first I was like, this is probably just&nbsp;
a noise artifact. But as the data kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:56.100
coming in and the peak kept getting clearer&nbsp;
as it was like, “This is is a wormhole.”

00:11:56.100 --> 00:12:00.900
“I think I just saw one.” And so I was like&nbsp;
sending it off to Maria and the others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:02.880
like, “I think we have a wormhole, guys.”

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:07.260
He said, "It's there." I&nbsp;
said, "No kidding it's there."

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:09.780
It was nuts. It was nuts.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.260
The qubits of the Google quantum computer are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.620
making a little bit of extra&nbsp;
space and that's our wormhole.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:26.340
What happens is, a pulse of negative&nbsp;
energy falls into the wormhole.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:32.100
Now, the wormhole becomes traversable. It opens.

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:35.480
You put a qubit through one side of the wormhole.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:39.000
The qubit is now in the interior of this wormhole.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:46.260
That information will actually spread over&nbsp;
the entire quantum system over time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:48.420
this spread of quantum information is chaotic.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:52.500
It becomes shared by many particles in the&nbsp;
form of very complicated quantum entanglement.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:57.780
After spreading, it has to&nbsp;
refocus onto a single qubit.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.880
As the wormhole is closing, the qubit is exiting.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:13.260
I was absolutely astounded. I thought we would get&nbsp;
maybe a few steps toward a real wormhole on a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:20.783
quantum computer. And I never really believed that&nbsp;
we'd get all the way to something that was real.

00:13:20.783 --> 00:13:23.460
The experimentalists have a reaction&nbsp;
like they want to hug the machine.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:32.220
I told my brother and he squealed and he&nbsp;
said, "Are you destroying the world? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:38.340
are you doing?" It was difficult to explain to&nbsp;
him that nobody is in danger from this wormhole.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:45.240
On the processor, if we had a&nbsp;
quantum eye and looked in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:48.540
we wouldn't see a ripple of space-time.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:52.080
We would see the physics that we expect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.320
the gravity point of view and&nbsp;
from the quantum point of view.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:01.260
So if that is the case, then we are making&nbsp;
contact between quantum and gravity.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.960
That's been the holy grail of my whole&nbsp;
career–is to understand quantum gravity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:08.760
and the things that go with it, like wormholes.

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:12.600
We are studying quantum gravity&nbsp;
literally from the ground up.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:22.680
Einstein committed his life to arrive to&nbsp;
some understanding of quantum gravity, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:25.260
here we are almost a century later.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:28.800
I wish he was alive to see this because&nbsp;
I would like to know what he would say.

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:35.940
What would have Einstein said? He would've said,&nbsp;
“ER = EPR. I told you. I knew that in 1935.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:37.740
But he didn't.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:44.700
I think this is concrete&nbsp;
experimental evidence for ER = EPR.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:49.380
The vector is pointing right from&nbsp;
the experimental point of view.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:58.440
This is a confirmation–a direct exploration–of&nbsp;
this connection between quantum entanglement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:02.940
on the one hand and the way space is&nbsp;
knit together and holds itself together.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:08.460
As Google builds more and more capable&nbsp;
quantum computers, we will eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:13.260
be able to get this into a regime where we don't&nbsp;
even know how to predict what's going to happen.

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:16.140
And I think we're going to have&nbsp;
hundreds of my fellow physicists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.540
now writing papers about all the&nbsp;
other things you can think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.960
doing now–now that we've sort of&nbsp;
got this door a little bit open.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:27.960
And as always in physics, when you&nbsp;
are able to explore a new frontier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:32.820
there will be discoveries that weren't&nbsp;
anticipated. Just like when you build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:38.460
more powerful accelerator to study particle&nbsp;
collisions or a more powerful telescope to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:42.900
look at the more distant universe, a&nbsp;
more powerful quantum computer will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:47.040
able to study highly entangled matter that&nbsp;
we've never been able to look at before.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:56.460
Being very close with quantum,&nbsp;
we're going to learn a lot.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:04.140
There’s so many questions that one&nbsp;
could explore using these ideas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:10.500
And I think most exciting are the&nbsp;
questions that we can’t yet pose.

